  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 211 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nWith the upper-level ridge shifting to the east of the Mid-\nMississippi River Valley on Wednesday, upper-level flow will become \nsouthwesterly with two shortwave troughs navigating this flow. The \nfirst shortwave trough will track over the Northern/Central Plains \ninto Upper Midwest late Wednesday but too far northwest of the CWA \nto have an impact other than passing, thin upper-level clouds and \nenhancement of low-level WAA with temperatures warming further above \naverage including highs in the 60s to around 70 F and lows in the \n40s to low-50s F.\n\nOn Saturday, global model guidance is in agreement that the second \nshortwave trough will follow a more southeasterly track than its \npredecessor with a stronger attendant cold front that advances \nsoutheastward through the CWA late Saturday. Ensemble model guidance \nprobabilities of measurable rainfall are 70 to 90 percent in a 24-\nhour period centered on Saturday evening, but it is worth noting \nthat probabilities are highest in northeastern MO with large-scale \nascent and the strength of the front likely decreasing with \nsoutheastward extent. Probabilities of 0.25\"+ rainfall are only 20 \nto 30 percent, suggesting that significant relief from ongoing \nabnormally dry and drought conditions is unlikely. Behind the front, \nNBM temperature distributions have been slightly contracting toward \nnear to slightly below average Sunday into early next week, but the \ninterquartile range is still around 8 F in that timeperiod.",
